course: politics
lesson: 13
title: Memes, clips and jokes about politicians
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A meme is like a joke passed round the playground at top speed: a picture, a few words, and in an hour everyone has seen it. It is funny, but it is one picture, and a whole story never fits in one picture.
explain: People have laughed at leaders for hundreds of years, with cartoons, impressions and fake news shows called satire. Laughing at what the powerful do can help keep them honest, and you can laugh at a leader and still like them.
explain: But a joke can turn into a lie once it loses its label, a six-second clip can hide what came before and after, and a computer can now make a fake video that looks real.
explain: So the rule is simple: laugh, then check. Look for the whole video, the real source and the real quote, and joke about what people do, never about how they look.
explain: Kids text about clips in their own slang: "sus" means suspicious, "mid" means just average, "it's giving" means "it looks like", "I'm dead" means "that's so funny", and in Scotland "aye" means yes and "wee" means small.
fact: A meme is a funny picture or video with words, called a caption, that gets copied and shared; it cannot explain a whole story.
fact: Satire is comedy that pokes fun at the powerful to make a point; a parody is a funny copy of something real, like a fake news show.
fact: An impression copies how a famous person talks and moves; comedians in many countries have mocked leaders for hundreds of years.
fact: Joking about what someone does is different from joking about their body or looks, which says nothing about their work and hurts people who look the same.
fact: Joking about people with more power than you is called punching up.
fact: A short clip cut out of a long video can change what it seems to show; this is called taking it out of context.
fact: Political cartoons make a point about the news with drawings; a caricature stretches someone's features, and leaders are often drawn as animals.
fact: A fake quote is words someone never said made to look real; jokes from satire accounts can become lies once shared without their label.
fact: A deepfake is a fake video or voice made by a computer to look real; odd details and checking a trusted news site help you spot one.
fact: When a clip goes viral, news shows sometimes chase it, which can push more important stories off the screen.
fact: Politicians use jokes too, and many have social media teams who make memes and videos for them.
fact: Memes and comedy can spark curiosity, but you need to know the real story to understand the joke.
fact: People disagree whether memes get young people interested in politics or squash big topics into jokes without facts.
exercise: What is a meme? | A funny picture or video with words that gets copied and shared | A long news report from a serious newspaper, printed every morning | A party's list of promises | A vote on one question
exercise: What is satire? | Comedy that pokes fun at the powerful to make a point | A serious news report about what the government did that day | A fact check of a speech | A government advert
exercise: A show looks like the evening news, but every story is a joke. What is it? | A satire or parody show | A real news bulletin, read out live | A party political advert | A live debate between two leaders
exercise: What is an impression? | Copying how someone talks and moves to make people laugh | A photo of a politician printed on the front page of a paper | A speech in parliament | A poll of viewers
exercise: Can you laugh at an impression of a leader and still like that leader? | Yes | No, laughing means you dislike them | Only if you did not vote | Only on their birthday
exercise: Which kind of joke says nothing about a politician's work? | A joke about their body or looks | A joke about a promise they broke | A joke about a law they passed last year | A joke about a speech they gave
exercise: What is punching up? | Joking about people with more power than you | Hitting someone in a debate | Making a meme go viral | Shouting at a TV
exercise: What does "out of context" mean? | Shown without what came before and after | Shown in full from start to end | Shown only on the news | Shown in a cartoon
exercise: What did the full video of the "sleeping" politician show? | He was looking down at his notes | He really was asleep | He was a robot | He had left the room
exercise: What is a caricature? | A drawing that stretches someone's features to make them funny | A photo with no changes at all, taken by a newspaper photographer | A list of facts | A short comedy scene on stage
exercise: Why do political cartoons often turn leaders into animals? | To show an idea about the news quickly | Because leaders like animals | Because photos are not allowed | To hide who the leader is
exercise: The "school on Saturdays" quote came from a joke page. What is it now that people share it as real? | A fake quote | A satire label on a joke | A fact check of a quote | A caricature in a paper
exercise: What is a deepfake? | A fake video or voice made by a computer to look real | A video filmed deep underwater by a special camera | A very old cartoon from the days before television | A real clip that was slowed down to look sleepy
exercise: What can happen when the news chases a viral clip? | More important stories get pushed off the screen | The clip becomes less popular | The news becomes more accurate, because viewers get to choose what really matters | The politician wins votes for sure
exercise: Why might a politician share a meme that makes fun of herself? | Laughing at yourself can make you seem friendly | It is a law that politicians must share all memes | She did not know that it was meant to be a joke at all | To stop people from voting for her rivals in the election
exercise: Why are memes alone not enough to know the news? | They leave out a lot and need you to already know the story | They are always false, because only people who hate politicians make them | They are too long to read | Only adults can see them
test: «this clip is SO sus, he's literally asleep at work lmao» (sus = suspicious). What should you do first? | Look for the whole video before deciding or sharing | Share it straight away, before anyone can delete it | Believe it, because short clips can never be cut up | Report him to the police for sleeping at his work
test: "Och, aye, it's only a wee joke about his belly" says a lad in Glasgow (aye = yes, wee = small). What is the problem? | Jokes about looks say nothing about his work and can hurt others | Nothing, body jokes are about his work, because a politician's belly shows how hard he works | It is not a joke, it is a fact | Belly jokes are against the law
test: Auntie shares the "Saturday school" quote: "Absolute disgrace, I'm livid!" What should she have done? | Checked where the quote came from before sharing | Shared it twice to be fair | Added an angry face | Written to the joke page
test: «the president singing that song?? I'm dead, it's real right» (I'm dead = that's so funny). The voice sounds odd. What is best? | Check a trusted news site; it may be a deepfake | It must be real, because it is a video and has sound | Videos can never be faked by anyone sitting at home | Forward it to everyone first, then check it later
test: Mum laughs at an actor copying the president she voted for. Your brother says she must hate him now. What is true? | You can laugh at an impression and still like the person | Laughing means she will never vote for him again, whatever he does after this | Impressions are only made of hated leaders | Laughing at a leader is against the law
test: "That cartoon with two cats, it's giving primary school" (it's giving = it looks like). Grandad says it is about two leaders arguing. What is a political cartoon? | An opinion about the news told with drawings | A children's story about cats | A photo of real cats | A fact check in pictures
test: A tripping clip gets two million views and the news leads with it. Your mate says it must be the biggest story. What is wrong? | Popular is not the same as important | The most viewed story is always the most important | The news only ever shows true stories | Tripping over is a political decision
test: A comedy show copies the news with a straight face. What would happen if someone thought it was real news? | They could believe jokes as if they were facts | Nothing, satire is always true | They would learn every fact correctly | The show would become real news
test: Aroha's brother: "news is mid, I just get it from memes" (mid = average). What is the best advice? | Let a joke lead you to the facts, not replace them | Memes tell the whole story, because they squeeze all the news into one picture | Memes are always more accurate | Never laugh at memes
test: A politician posts a meme mocking herself with "Fair enough!" What is worth remembering? | A joke from a politician is still part of her campaign | Politicians cannot make jokes | It means she will quit politics and become a comedian on the telly instead | Her team had nothing to do with it
test: Some people say mocking leaders is a proud tradition of free countries. What do others worry about? | Constant mocking makes people stop respecting the job, not just the person | Mocking is never allowed anywhere | Comedians are secretly paid by the leaders they mock, so the jokes are all adverts | Jokes make leaders too powerful
exam: Identify the term which denotes the presentation of a brief excerpt of a recording without that which preceded and followed it. | Out of context | Satire | Parody | Punching up
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a deepfake? | A video or voice recording fabricated by a computer so as to appear genuine | A genuine recording reproduced at reduced speed so as to give a misleading impression of the speaker | A drawing which exaggerates the features of a person | A comedy programme imitating a news bulletin
exam: Select the response consistent with the established principle concerning humour directed at politicians. | Humour addressing the conduct of a person differs from humour addressing that person's appearance | Humour addressing appearance constitutes a legitimate form of fact checking concerning the conduct of politicians | All humour concerning politicians is prohibited | Humour concerning politicians is invariably accurate
exam: Whereas a fabricated quotation originates from a satire account and is subsequently shared without its label, identify the most appropriate course of action. | The origin of the quotation shall be verified prior to sharing | The quotation shall be shared without delay | The quotation shall be accepted as genuine | The quotation shall be attributed to the satire account's readers
